  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSatellite imagery shows the surface low continuing \nto slowly move eastward offshore the Mid Atlantic. Associated \nlingering rain and snow showers are rapidly tapering off, with \na few flurries or sprinkles lingering primarily south of I-90 \nearly this afternoon. Winter Weather Advisories have been \nallowed to expire. Please refer to Public Information Statements\nfor snowfall totals from this event.\n\nAn upper level trough will remain overhead through this weekend, \ncontinuing the trend of below normal temperatures. High \ntemperatures will remain in the 30s and 40s with overnight lows \ndropping into the 20s and 30s. Cyclonic flow and enduring low \nlevel moisture will bring isolated to scattered rain/snow \nshowers on Saturday afternoon and evening, primarily for areas \nin the western Adirondacks, western Mohawk Valley, southern \nGreens, and eastern Catskills due to upsloping. Any snow \naccumulations will be light and primarily be confined to higher \nelevations. The coverage of showers will decrease going into \nSunday morning, with mostly dry conditions expected by Sunday \nafternoon across eastern New York and western New England.\n\nThe aforementioned trough will begin to depart from the region \nby Sunday evening, with a large area of high pressure moving in \nfrom the west behind it. This will allow for dry weather \nthroughout next week, with the gradual introduction of above \nnormal temperatures by mid-week, continuing through the end of \nthe week.",
